LLM-BASED PLAYER FEEDBACK SYSTEM
================================

The LLM system provides contextual feedback to the player based on the current flood-game state. Unity first calculates and validates the gameplay data, then sends the validated information to a locally running language model.

The system uses:
- Unity C# scripts for gameplay data and calculations
- LM Studio for running the LLM locally
- Phi-3.1 Mini 4K Instruct model
- Local API communication between Unity and LM Studio
- Persona-based feedback generation

Local LLM Endpoint -> http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1/chat/completions
Model -> phi-3.1-mini-4k-instruct


LLM PROCESSING FLOW
===================

When feedback is requested:
1. Unity collects the current gameplay information.
2. A C# Data Builder calculates and validates numerical values.
3. The validated facts are passed to LLMManager.
4. LLMManager creates the LLM request.
5. The request is sent to the local LM Studio server.
6. Phi-3.1 generates player-facing feedback.
7. The response is returned to Unity.
8. LLMUIController displays the response to the player.

Unity Game State
      |
      v
C# Data Builder
      |
      v
Validated Game Facts
      |
      v
LLMManager
      |
      v
LM Studio
      |
      v
Phi-3.1 Mini
      |
      v
LLM Feedback
      |
      v
LLMUIController

The numerical calculations are performed in C# rather than by the LLM. This reduces arithmetic errors and prevents the LLM from becoming the authoritative source for game-state values.


SHELTER FEEDBACK
================

The Shelter feedback system evaluates shelter capacity and population assignment.

Test data:
- Shelter S-01: Capacity 500, Assigned 650
- Shelter S-02: Capacity 600, Assigned 520

ShelterFeedbackDataBuilder calculates:
- S-01 excess population = 150
- S-02 available capacity = 80
- Possible transfer = 80
- Remaining excess = 70

The calculated values are passed to the LLM as authoritative facts.


EVACUATION FEEDBACK
===================

The Evacuation feedback system evaluates evacuation progress and route availability.

Test data:
- Available routes = 3
- Selected routes = 2
- Blocked routes = 1
- Total population = 1200
- Evacuated population = 850

EvacuationFeedbackDataBuilder calculates:
- Usable routes = 2
- Remaining population = 350
- Selected routes exceed usable routes = False

The validated evacuation information is then passed to LLMManager for feedback generation.


FLOOD DEFENSE FEEDBACK
======================

The Flood Defense feedback system is connected to the actual Unity flood-defense gameplay system.

FloodDefenseFeedbackDataBuilder reads information from FloodDefenseBoxStamp.

The system uses:
- BarriersPlaced
- ActualMaxZoneBarriers

It calculates:
- Number of barriers placed
- Maximum barriers available
- Number of barriers remaining
- Whether all available barriers have been placed

Example:
Barriers Placed: 3
Maximum Available: 3
Barriers Remaining: 0
All Barriers Placed: True


AUTOMATIC FLOOD DEFENSE LLM TRIGGER
===================================

FloodDefenseLLMTrigger automatically generates feedback when the player finishes placing all available flood-defense barriers.

The FloodDefenseBoxStamp script invokes OnAllZoneBarriersPlaced when the final barrier is committed.

FloodDefenseLLMTrigger listens for this event.

Player Places Barrier
        |
        v
FloodDefenseBoxStamp
        |
        v
3 / 3 Barriers Placed
        |
        v
OnAllZoneBarriersPlaced
        |
        v
FloodDefenseLLMTrigger
        |
        v
FloodDefenseFeedbackDataBuilder
        |
        v
LLMManager
        |
        v
Phi-3.1 Feedback

Therefore, the player does not need to press a keyboard test button for the final flood-defense implementation.


MANUAL TESTING CONTROLS
=======================

During development, keyboard controls were used to test individual feedback modules.

F -> Shelter Feedback
E -> Evacuation Feedback
B -> Flood Defense Feedback

These controls are primarily for testing. The current Flood Defense implementation can generate feedback automatically after all barriers are placed.


LLM UI
======

LLMUIController handles displaying the generated response inside Unity.

The UI contains:

LLMUIController
        |
        +-- LLMFeedbackPanel
              |
              +-- LLMFeedbackText
              |
              +-- CloseButton

ShowMessage() displays the generated LLM response.
HideMessage() closes the feedback panel.


IMPORTANT LLM DESIGN RULE
=========================

The LLM should not calculate important gameplay values.

The implemented architecture is:

Game Data
   |
   v
C# Calculation + Validation
   |
   v
Authoritative Facts
   |
   v
LLM
   |
   v
Natural-Language Feedback

This design was adopted because the LLM occasionally produced incorrect arithmetic or unsupported information such as road conditions, water levels, barrier effectiveness, or protected zones.

The prompt therefore instructs the model to treat Unity-calculated values as authoritative and avoid inventing information that is not contained in the supplied game state.


